WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:11.004
The Common Picture is a standing, public picture of the lives people want.

2
00:00:11.004 --> 00:00:14.226
It's built from private conversations in people's

3
00:00:14.226 --> 00:00:17.320
own language, and tested by people drawn by lot.

4
00:00:18.022 --> 00:00:21.872
It asks institutions for one thing: answer it in public.

5
00:00:22.573 --> 00:00:25.608
This is a demonstration, on invented examples.

6
00:00:25.608 --> 00:00:29.094
Every person, host and number you'll see is invented.

7
00:00:29.094 --> 00:00:32.193
And "The Common Picture" is only a working name.

8
00:00:37.001 --> 00:00:40.463
First, a private conversation about a real day.

9
00:00:40.463 --> 00:00:42.771
Choose a language, and a way in.

10
00:00:43.471 --> 00:00:46.931
The interviewer says it's an AI, not a person.

11
00:00:46.931 --> 00:00:50.391
Then it asks about yesterday, not your opinions.

12
00:00:51.092 --> 00:00:53.012
We'll use invented example answers.

13
00:00:55.878 --> 00:00:58.188
Now say something general, like "money".

14
00:01:01.163 --> 00:01:03.951
It doesn't argue, or give a view.

15
00:01:03.951 --> 00:01:07.313
It asks when money showed up in your day.

16
00:01:08.014 --> 00:01:10.324
We'll answer the rest with examples.

17
00:01:16.442 --> 00:01:20.861
Then it says: here is what I heard, in your own words.

18
00:01:20.861 --> 00:01:23.752
You keep, change or drop each point.

19
00:01:30.327 --> 00:01:35.327
Then you choose whether to add them to the picture for your place.

20
00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.340
In this demonstration, nothing is stored.

21
00:01:45.194 --> 00:01:47.812
There's an ordinary phone call too.

22
00:01:47.812 --> 00:01:50.574
This part's a mock-up, and it says so.

23
00:01:51.274 --> 00:01:55.386
You ring and hang up, and it calls you back, free.

24
00:01:55.386 --> 00:02:01.274
It reads back what it heard, and you keep or change each point by keypad.

25
00:02:06.104 --> 00:02:08.388
The picture opens on the world layer,

26
00:02:08.388 --> 00:02:11.874
built from a hundred and sixty-two invented conversations.

27
00:02:12.575 --> 00:02:16.141
You can pick a layer: five places, and one circle.

28
00:02:16.141 --> 00:02:21.035
Every layer has the same five parts, built upward, never by averaging.

29
00:02:21.737 --> 00:02:24.613
Part one is what's said almost everywhere.

30
00:02:24.613 --> 00:02:27.154
Two counts travel with every finding:

31
00:02:27.154 --> 00:02:30.967
the share of people, and the share of places and circles.

32
00:02:31.668 --> 00:02:33.899
Counts come through two doors.

33
00:02:33.899 --> 00:02:37.353
The open door is anyone who chose to take part.

34
00:02:37.353 --> 00:02:40.518
The drawn sample is people picked at random.

35
00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.094
Nothing is reweighted quietly.

36
00:02:43.094 --> 00:02:45.450
This finding rests on the drawn sample.

37
00:02:46.151 --> 00:02:50.381
Open it, and you see the original words, with labelled translations.

38
00:02:51.082 --> 00:02:55.486
Part two, said differently, is the same need taking different shapes.

39
00:02:55.486 --> 00:02:58.002
Each statement is written from one side.

40
00:02:58.703 --> 00:03:01.910
Part three, said here, keeps a standing statement whole.

41
00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:05.623
This one's from an invented Quechua-speaking community near Cusco.

42
00:03:06.325 --> 00:03:08.332
It shows how one would appear.

43
00:03:08.332 --> 00:03:12.475
Real ones are written by communities, through their own process.

44
00:03:13.175 --> 00:03:19.152
Part four is in tension: a grower's fair price is a city family's food bill.

45
00:03:19.152 --> 00:03:22.025
And part five is who's not yet heard.

46
00:03:22.725 --> 00:03:26.955
Every part has a button: this is not what was said.

47
00:03:27.656 --> 00:03:29.966
In this demonstration, nothing is sent.

48
00:03:30.669 --> 00:03:35.575
Back at the top, a retention test checks that minority themes survive.

49
00:03:35.575 --> 00:03:36.819
Seven of eight do.

50
00:03:37.521 --> 00:03:42.042
Switch to Kisumu, and it's the same five parts for one place,

51
00:03:42.042 --> 00:03:45.981
with a right of reply from an invented traders' group.

52
00:03:50.791 --> 00:03:54.149
Good days from elsewhere are short and ordinary.

53
00:03:54.149 --> 00:03:57.711
No names, no rankings, and nothing to like or share.

54
00:03:58.412 --> 00:04:00.332
Here are days about care.

55
00:04:05.887 --> 00:04:10.890
In a slow room, seven people drawn by lot work through one tension.

56
00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.347
It stays open for days, and it decides nothing.

57
00:04:15.048 --> 00:04:18.641
You write where you stand before you read the others.

58
00:04:18.641 --> 00:04:23.898
Then their messages appear in your language, with the original beside each one.

59
00:04:24.598 --> 00:04:26.908
Talk it through is private.

60
00:04:26.908 --> 00:04:29.218
It gives no view of its own.

61
00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:34.919
Say you agree, and it shows you reasons from people who answered differently.

62
00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:40.240
Then it asks what, if anything, is true in them for you.

63
00:04:45.053 --> 00:04:50.220
Institutions are asked to pledge to answer the picture in public, by a date.

64
00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.905
Every host here is an invented example.

65
00:04:52.905 --> 00:04:55.053
No real institution has pledged.

66
00:04:55.754 --> 00:04:58.834
Like this invented county government in western Kenya.

67
00:04:59.534 --> 00:05:03.384
Each ask comes with signs an ordinary person could check.

68
00:05:04.084 --> 00:05:05.919
The asks are invented too.

69
00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:08.704
No assembly has met, or decided anything.

70
00:05:09.406 --> 00:05:14.629
The register shows every answer, and counts the days when one is late.

71
00:05:14.629 --> 00:05:17.866
And people drawn by lot check what was done.

72
00:05:22.691 --> 00:05:26.404
Rehema is an invented example, with a basic phone.

73
00:05:26.404 --> 00:05:30.771
She rings a number from the radio, and gets a free callback.

74
00:05:31.472 --> 00:05:37.242
Later it reads back four points, and she keeps or changes them with the keypad.

75
00:05:42.055 --> 00:05:44.745
Every page has a Give feedback button.

76
00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:47.851
It says who can read what you send:

77
00:05:47.851 --> 00:05:51.595
the people building this demonstration, and nobody else.

78
00:05:52.297 --> 00:05:55.928
Answer a few guided questions, in about four minutes.

79
00:05:55.928 --> 00:05:57.677
Or just tell us something.

80
00:05:58.376 --> 00:06:01.836
You can send what you've said at any point.

81
00:06:03.386 --> 00:06:06.466
Send it, and you'll get a thank you.

82
00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:10.570
The About page lists what this demonstration doesn't show.

83
00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.936
Like whether any institution will answer.

84
00:06:13.636 --> 00:06:16.716
It's a demonstration of method, and nothing more.
